## 2. Documented deployments — what has actually shipped, and what it produced

**Dubai International (DXB), 2010.** Cut daily announcements across three terminals from >2,000 to <700, capped at two announcements per flight (boarding call and final call), and retained paging only for gates, lounges, transfer desks, and emergencies. CEO Paul Griffiths states there was "no impact on departure performance," a self-reported claim with no independent audit that we could locate [Source: https://media.dubaiairports.ae/dubai-international-gives-passengers-the-silent-treatment/]. The DXB implementation is the most-cited "silent airport" reference; every subsequent adopter uses the same architecture (localized boarding paging, killed general-terminal paging).

**London City Airport (LCY), 2008–present.** Under an explicit "silent terminal" policy since 2008. No flight or gate announcements over the PA; passengers rely on FIDS and receive direct real-time push via Facebook Messenger, Twitter, and airline apps. Emergency and safety announcements remain. LCY's Director of Customer Experience Melanie Burnley: "From 2008, the airport implemented a 'silent terminal' policy, which means only essential announcements are delivered via the public-address system" [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. LCY has a single-carrier-heavy, business-traveler profile, small footprint, no international transfer product — the most favorable case in the reference set.

**Helsinki-Vantaa (HEL), Finavia — June 2019.** Formal "Silent Airport" project with airline partners: no flight or passenger announcements in the terminal; announcements retained at the gate and for emergencies. Finavia's own 2016 passenger study reported that passengers considered the policy "more positive than neutral" on comfort, "lower[ed] stress levels," and rated wayfinding difficulty as "neutral" — i.e., no measured improvement in wayfinding, only no measured harm [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over].

**Copenhagen (CPH).** Runs a "silent airport" policy that minimizes voice announcements and adds a designed inventory of silent lounges and sound-dampened zones near Gates A4, A10, and C15, plus silent pods on Terminal 3 Level 3. No consolidated operational metrics located in the time budget; passenger-guide sources are the primary documentation [Source: https://copenhagenlocaltours.com/copenhagen-airport-guide-transport-layover/].

**Amsterdam Schiphol.** Frequently listed as a "silent airport" in trade press but this brief could not find a Schiphol operator document authorizing an announcement-reduction policy comparable to DXB's or HEL's. Schiphol's public noise agenda is dominated by *aircraft* noise reduction (community, not terminal). Treat the "Schiphol silent airport" label with skepticism until an operator source is found; the label appears mostly in aggregator lists [Source: https://www.pte-world.com/exhibitor-news/the-silent-airport-guide-unveiling-the-path-to-peaceful-passenger-experiences].

**San Francisco International (SFO), 2018–2020.** The single strongest US reference and the only deployment in the peer set with numeric before/after data.

- Individual paging occurrences: 492 → 261 (**–47 %**).
- Cumulative paging duration: 145 min/day → 58 min/day (**–60 %**).
- On-site noise reduction (self-measured): **>40 %**.
- Method: zoned paging software across four terminals (SFO cites 21,000 m² of paging zones); banned automated announcements except TSA/security/safety; banned stereo systems at boarding gates; physically disabled airport-wide paging capability; decibel caps in concessions [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_dec20/san_francisco_international_airport_noise].
- Christopher Birch, SFO Director of Guest Experience: "We have Americans with Disabilities Act requirements here and that's going to be the tough one to incorporate" — the only US operator statement in this evidence set that publicly names accessibility as an *unresolved* engineering problem, not a solved one [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_dec20/san_francisco_international_airport_noise].

**Pittsburgh International (PIT), 2019.** "Presley's Place," a 1,500 sq ft engineered sensory suite with a mock jetway and cabin. Reported total cost **$930,390**, funded through the Allegheny County Airport Authority Charitable Foundation, American Airlines, Edith L. Trees Charitable Trust, Magee Plastics, and Hillman Family Foundations, with donated equipment [Source: https://www.parentingspecialneeds.org/article/pittsburgh-intl-airport-unveils-the-largest-most-comprehensive-airport-sensory-space-in-the-world/]. This is the reference cost for a purpose-built airport sensory space at ~$620/sq ft. Hartsfield-Jackson opened a smaller Delta-partnered multisensory room in 2016; no consolidated cost figure located [Source: https://www.todaysparent.com/family/special-needs/autism-friendly-airports/].

**Munich (MUC).** Frequently listed as "quiet" but this brief could not locate a formal announcement-reduction policy at the terminal level; MUC's public "quiet" story is night-flight restriction, an airside/noise-abatement matter unrelated to terminal PA. Treat the MUC label the same way as Schiphol's: unverified [Source: https://www.munich-airport.com/night-flight-264466].

