## 3. What is off-the-shelf in 2026 vs. what still requires custom work

**Commodity in 2026 (buy, don't build):**

- Zoned PA / EVACS platforms with IP-addressable zones (Bosch Praesensa, Bosch Praesideo end-of-life migrations, Siemens Cerberus PRO Voice, Notifier ONYXWorks Voice, TOA Voice Alarm, Baldwin BoxCast broadcast integrations). All support STIPA-tested outputs and are the working default in new US terminals since roughly 2018.
- STIPA measurement for NFPA 72 commissioning (NTi Audio XL2 with STIPA, Bedrock Audio testers). This is standard commissioning practice in the US. Independent commissioning agents run it; costs are known and small relative to the EVACS itself [Source: https://www.nti-audio.com/Portals/0/data/en/NTi-Audio-AppNote-STIPA-Measurement.pdf].
- Directional loudspeakers (Panphonics, Holosonics Audio Spotlight, Focused Audio) for queues, information desks, and single-gate boarding calls. Panphonics claims "thousands of use cases globally" and lists airports as a core segment [Source: https://panphonics.com/]. Verified airport case studies for these vendors are thinner than the marketing implies — Heathrow's "Passport to Poetry" installation used Audio Spotlight (Holosonics), not Panphonics [Source: https://www.directionalaudio.co.uk/sectors/case-study-heathrow-airport/]. Deployment is commodity; *airport-specific* case evidence is not.
- FIDS with mobile push. Every US Category X airline (United, American, Delta, Alaska, Southwest) already sends real-time gate and boarding notifications via app; every FIDS vendor (Amadeus, SITA, Ink, Rockwell Collins/Collins Aerospace ARINC vMUSE) supports API push into airport apps. The plumbing exists.
- Absorbent ceiling and wall systems specified against target reverberation times. The empirical guidance in the peer-reviewed literature is T30 ≤ 3.5 s, STI ≥ 0.45, background NC ≤ 55 in large terminal volumes [Source: https://doi.org/10.3390/buildings15060915]. Meeting these targets is a materials-and-geometry design problem, not a technology-availability problem.

**Still custom / integration-heavy in 2026:**

- **Governance tooling.** A page-analytics dashboard that counts announcement frequency, duration, dB, and zone across airline signatories, ground handlers, and TSA and reports monthly on compliance with a written standard — no US operator we could source runs this off a commercial shelf. SFO built its own operational discipline; there is no obvious out-of-box package.
- **Accessible redundant notification.** Push notifications require a smartphone, an installed app, a data connection, and the passenger's attention. There is no commodity system in 2026 that guarantees notification to a passenger without those four preconditions. Visual FIDS + gate-agent human channel remains the fallback. This is the load-bearing gap SFO's Birch names above.
- **Multilingual and low-vision accessibility layered onto push.** Dubai Airports announced automated 25-language PA announcements in 2019 [Source: https://www.khaleejtimes.com/business/aviation/dubai-airports-to-make-automated-announcements-in-25-languages]; the equivalent multilingual coverage on push channels is *not* standardized in US carrier apps.
- **Beacon / geofenced targeted audio-visual at seat level.** Piloted in retail; not deployed at scale in US terminals. Treat as R&D.
- **Historic-building acoustic retrofit.** Retrofitting Saarinen's IAD Main Terminal or Goodman's DCA Terminal 1 to a modern target T30/STI is a custom acoustic engineering project every time, not a product purchase.

