## 5. Honest maturity assessment: real vs. hype

**Real in 2026.**

- Zoned PA, EVACS-integrated intelligibility measurement, absorbent architectural acoustics, mobile push through airline apps, directional loudspeakers at fixed points, and engineered sensory rooms are all deployable off documented product lines, verified by third-party commissioning, and defensible in a construction spec.
- The SFO metrics (47 % / 60 % reduction with no reported IROPS harm through 2020) are the strongest US signal that a policy-plus-zoning combination works at scale in a large hub.

**Real but self-reported.**

- Every "no impact on departure performance" claim from an operator (DXB, HEL) is a marketing statement. Independent audit — say, a comparison of gate-arrival compliance, boarding on-time, or missed-boarding rates against a matched pre-policy baseline — has not been published for any peer airport we could source. A strategist should discount those claims accordingly.

**Hype in 2026.**

- "Silent airport" as a passenger-experience brand. Copenhagen's tourism press treats it as a product feature; the underlying technology is unremarkable.
- Beacon-driven personalized audio ("your gate change, spoken to you and only you at your seat") is technically feasible and not deployed at hub-airport scale. If it appears in a proposal, treat as pilot risk.
- "AI-driven announcement optimization." Generic label, no proven airport product with independent operational evidence in the sources reviewed.

**Regulatory floor that nobody markets.**

- NFPA 72 STI thresholds and 14 CFR Part 139 § 139.325 (Airport Emergency Plan communications network) constrain any MWAA standard. Neither is discretionary. The MWAA Design Manual acoustics section (which context builder flagged as not retrieved) will need to reconcile the quiet standard with these floors explicitly.

