## 6. Quotes and data points a strategist can use verbatim

1. "From 2008, the airport implemented a 'silent terminal' policy, which means only essential announcements are delivered via the public-address system." — Melanie Burnley, Director of Customer Experience, London City Airport [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over].

2. "We have Americans with Disabilities Act requirements here and that's going to be the tough one to incorporate." — Christopher Birch, Director of Guest Experience, SFO [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_dec20/san_francisco_international_airport_noise].

3. SFO Quiet Airport program (2018–2020): individual paging occurrences fell from 492/day to 261/day (–47 %); cumulative paging duration fell from 145 min/day to 58 min/day (–60 %); overall on-site noise reduction >40 % [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_dec20/san_francisco_international_airport_noise].

4. Dubai International reduced daily announcements from >2,000 to <700 across three terminals — a limit of two announcements per flight — "with no impact on departure performance," per CEO Paul Griffiths [Source: https://media.dubaiairports.ae/dubai-international-gives-passengers-the-silent-treatment/].

5. NFPA 72 requires "at least 90 percent of the measurement locations within each acoustically distinguishable space have a measured STI of not less than 0.45 and an average STI of not less than 0.50" for emergency communication systems [Source: https://sdifire.com/ensuring-clear-communication/].

6. Presley's Place at Pittsburgh International — 1,500 sq ft engineered sensory suite, total project cost **$930,390**, funded predominantly through charitable partners [Source: https://www.parentingspecialneeds.org/article/pittsburgh-intl-airport-unveils-the-largest-most-comprehensive-airport-sensory-space-in-the-world/].

