## 7. Direct implications for IAD and DCA

- **Concourse E (opens autumn 2026)** is the immediate test case. It is greenfield airside space, low-vault contemporary geometry, single dominant carrier (United). The full off-the-shelf quiet-by-design stack — zoned Bosch/Siemens-class EVACS with STIPA commissioning, absorbent ceiling systems targeting T30 ≤ 2.0 s, active-plus-adjacent-gate paging rule, directional units at info desks, FIDS density plus United-app push, sensory room with a proper capital line — is deliverable inside the existing construction package if MWAA writes the spec before commissioning. This window will not repeat cheaply at the next concourse.
- **DCA Terminal 2 (Pelli, 1997)** is the hardest case in the MWAA portfolio for quiet-by-design because the vaulted National Hall geometry drives long reverberation. Any single MWAA-wide STI/T30 threshold will either be trivial for Concourse E or infeasible for T2. The standard should specify a framework (measurement method, review gates, tenant obligations) with terminal-specific numeric targets.
- **IAD Saarinen Main Terminal and DCA Terminal 1 (Goodman)** — historic-preservation review will constrain acoustic retrofit. Custom acoustic engineering, Section 106 consultation, and negotiated targets, not standard products.
- **The 2025 Airline Use and Lease Agreement is the enforcement instrument.** Anything mandated on airline signatory paging behavior — active-plus-adjacent gate rule, script length caps, decibel caps at gate speakers, tenant sound-footprint rules — has to travel through the U&L and tenant leases, not through a unilateral operator rule. This is where SFO's approach translates and where DXB's does not: DXB is an operator/state model, MWAA is a signatory-carrier model. Any recommendation that ignores that difference will not survive contact with airline counsel.

